00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.750 align:start position:0%
 
hey  congratulations how's it feel to finally  but be back on solid ground  it's uh it's kind of strange to be home  again after such a long time  i'm just happy to be back with my family  back in my pets it's been great  it took much longer than we were  anticipating when we first spoke to you  it did so i was supposed to be back in  mid-october and then i was stuck in  alaska for a month i was stuck in russia  for a month because of visa and weather  problems but i'm finally back it took a  bit longer than i was hoping but yeah  and with two guinness world records is  that right tell me about them  that's right so i am now the youngest  woman to fly sail around the world and  the youngest person to fly certainly  around the world in microlight so it's  basically an extra small plane with a  few extra like limitations  what um what inspired you to do this in  the first place  so at first i'd always dreamt of flying  around the world but i just assumed that  it'd be too complicated too dangerous  too expensive so i never really thought  much about it and then i was finished  finishing school and i thought actually  this is the perfect time to do something  crazy and fly around the world so i  planned the route which basically  consisted of me grabbing a pen and a  world map and just kind of drawing where  i wanted to go  and then the next big step was finding  sponsorship so this whole trip has been  self-funded and then icdsoft a web  hosting company from bulgaria came on  board and helped support me  what inspired you as i said um you  talked about the fact that you felt that  you couldn't do it  or it was going to be too expensive and  then when you were  taking part in the journey what inspired  you to continue on because there must  have been moments when you thought i  can't do this i've had enough  definitely i think alaska and russia  were really tricky every flight was  really challenging whether it was  turbulence or just poor weather  and it really helped sometimes i get  messages from people saying uh zara you  know i saw what you were doing and  you've inspired me to start flying as  well and that was great so that would  really kind of um help me to keep going  because i thought actually i'm having a  bit of a positive impact on some people  so clearly i'm doing something right  were there any particularly memorable  parts of the journey when you were about  to take off or land or you were up and  it was just you and and very little  noise around you looking down and  thinking yeah this is cool  oh hundred percent i flew every active  volcano in iceland which was incredible  as you could kind of see the lava  flowing out that was amazing marines  beautiful siberia was fascinating  because i would fly for hundreds of  miles without seeing anything human  or human-made so no roads no electricity  cables no villages obviously it was  quite scary though because if the engine  were to stop suddenly i'm stuck in minus  35 degrees celsius hours away from  rescue so that was quite scary and then  some of the more political stuff so i  had to fly along  the border of north korean airspace  which was really interesting and quite  scary as well  but every country was gorgeous  what could you you might have been shot  down from north korea is that what  you're thinking right no so apparently  they test missiles uh with no warning  and although the risk is tiny there is a  risk so i thought well let's play it  safe and kind of fly along japanese  airspace rather than north korean  airspace i think that's very wise how  often did you manage to be in touch with  your family your mother's heart must  have been in her mouth the whole time  right yeah a little bit i think uh i  tried to explain them every single day  sometimes i was trickier in some  countries there was limited access to  wi-fi and internet  otherwise i had a satellite phone but  again in russia it was so cool like i  had to use it outside and because it was  so cool i could only phone my parents  for about five or ten minutes before my  my fingers would start to hurt  so what's next for you zara you're home  in brussels i'm sure that you had extra  big hugs when you got back um you need a  while to get over what's happened what's  next  for me the next big step will be getting  a degree probably i'd love to study  engineering at university and at the  same time hoping to you know keep talk  to people about my experiences and as  long as i can keep encouraging more  women to gain aviation right now only  five percent of uh commercial pilots are  women and i'm hoping to get that number  up  i think i knew that statistic already  it's unbelievable isn't it what about  going into space do you fancy that  that'd be pretty cool yeah i drew my  used to go to space one day but i need  to get a degree first and then that's  its own challenge so okay so what are  you gonna do did you say engineering  that's right engineering engineering  fantastic in brussels in belgium or in  the uk probably the uk or the us i'm  still waiting on the results so we'll  see what happens oh i'm sure you  absolutely nailed it it's good to talk  to you let us know how you get on won't  you  thank you  okay take care thank you

